            <ArticleTitle>Retinal capillaritis in a CRB1-associated retinal dystrophy.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>555-558</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1080/13816810.2017.1281966</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To report a case of CRB1-associated retinal dystrophy characterized by vitritis, retinal capillaritis, and cystoid macular edema (CME).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A case report.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">An 8-year-old boy was diagnosed with intermediate uveitis and treated with corticosteroids. He was subsequently diagnosed with retinal dystrophy and found to have two CRB1 mutations.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Retinal capillaritis, vitritis, and CME could be inflammatory features of CRB1 retinal dystrophy in our young patient.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
